#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129262 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128837 :question/id #uuid "acd76e31-5c7e-5577-a402-f003b3c2e1bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-11154" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves.\n\n$$\ne^y = \\dfrac{5}{e^x}, \\qquad e^y=\\dfrac{10}{e^x}, \\qquad e^x=e^y, \\qquad e^x=2e^y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as follows: \n\n![](images/f4/f47c1b7ce7e9073a10c2665821a4fc0d5b38ce6ad6c0f79a75a5b203962112cd.png)\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\ne^{x+y} = 5, \\qquad e^{x+y}=10, \\qquad e^{x-y} = 1, \\qquad e^{x-y} = 2\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu = e^{x+y}, \\qquad v = e^{x-y}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/88/88c92ebc206481c3ef86661ee413358bb060cb85f642148c609de0bf1a9da2d1.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=e^{x+y}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x+y}=5\\,⟹\\,u=5 \\\\  & {e}^{x+y}=10\\,⟹\\,u=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v =e^{x-y}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x-y}=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & {e}^{x-y}=2\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 5 \\leq u \\leq 10, \\quad 1 \\leq v \\leq 2\\big\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/105745a82e8f67b086f526b0166e3b442d3eaf02e4df69314ed9d3ffd5fca071.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T.$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1} =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-2x}=-\\dfrac{1}{2uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)}\\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$  In other words, $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2uv}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{uv}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c444d700-8c19-5712-8cf5-99555938b0c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ff3e8eb-7902-58a5-868e-63835438a0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126531} {:answer/id #uuid "93d7d291-7c2d-5519-b040-7f86626378b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126532} {:answer/id #uuid "e00d9ba3-0616-526a-918f-f048ec3ad32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126533} {:answer/id #uuid "e22b2d72-5d69-5a7c-b99b-a96070394e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126534} {:answer/id #uuid "04aac39f-4ad5-591b-8475-0d18f4471535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126535}] :answer-field/id #uuid "57c4cb66-dacd-5670-bc66-deebb81b0535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126530}] :question/id #uuid "d109551f-ec24-59b5-88d1-83b6f9c3f33e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160373" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n$y = \\frac{1}{x}, y = \\frac{2}{x}, y = x, y = 2x$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ada13f48-40f7-55cb-b292-90c5edb9db9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126561} {:answer/id #uuid "b4016fab-e55c-57c5-b2a5-734f2747fcf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126562} {:answer/id #uuid "c801c9f9-b6e2-51d8-ad3b-d69cd19c37e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126563} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d6470c-82f1-58a6-a766-266d161ce796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126564} {:answer/id #uuid "faacf412-63f3-5338-95d9-89827b2150b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126565}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2b71200b-d5f3-5b9b-914d-7c6c09ca6689" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126560} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9a5a2a-545e-5d46-b19c-4068b1213e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371894} {:answer/id #uuid "63280751-8551-52ab-aad4-1f353dc9cff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371895} {:answer/id #uuid "ae18d589-8d64-5813-919f-d918e476547f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371896} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4a2890-0cfb-55f6-8c16-0d5316c103ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371897} {:answer/id #uuid "901eef6c-1573-5902-bc29-0f98a541c427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63280751-8551-52ab-aad4-1f353dc9cff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371895} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc626db0-fa1b-5235-9810-17d15560c467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371893}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "846ced15-849c-5a63-8d00-4edf7b2a4931" :question/math-academy-id "q-160864" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n3y=5x+2,\\,3y=5x-2,\\,2x=y,\\,4x=2y+3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n5x-3y=-2,\\,5x-3y=2,\\,4x-2y=0,\\,4x-2y=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=5x-3y,\\,v=4x-2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=5x-3y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 5x-3y=-2\\,⟹\\,u=-2 \\\\  & 5x-3y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=4x-2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4x-2y=0\\,⟹\\,v=0 \\\\  & 4x-2y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/46/46b808722b24d467ba1015214268658e601779801ca54e8892b92b527ef9ee1d.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+12 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2-(-2))\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db65b2cf-8271-5607-a640-ae769caddf03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371874} {:answer/id #uuid "23bd3ecb-441d-5a27-8b9f-49934c6dbae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371875} {:answer/id #uuid "934e91be-6058-5dc5-ba33-86ee65e0a431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371876} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8bd3b1-0508-595d-9241-fa8262df8825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371877} {:answer/id #uuid "57283ea2-bea1-548c-804f-95b9c1485077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "934e91be-6058-5dc5-ba33-86ee65e0a431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371876} :answer-field/id #uuid "6938f289-182f-5501-8657-403464e01c47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff7a088d-dba6-5fcf-8b51-57e65b4c9e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-160654" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{3}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y=2,\\,{x}^{2}y=4,\\,xy=1,\\,xy=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}y,\\,v=xy\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2}y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}y=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & xy=3\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/ba/baa8e8ab6cff8edec84d3f0d0950931f247153523e3320abbdf673161afb44ae.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2dc75e0-ec1a-5f7b-8a43-e987a45ded5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371881} {:answer/id #uuid "31e24d23-144c-5f4c-83da-8b52dfbbc484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371882} {:answer/id #uuid "3b49b980-f639-5d69-be29-e580d2912e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371883} {:answer/id #uuid "055627e2-cfed-53bd-bcf2-ede52aba6dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371884} {:answer/id #uuid "55b60d42-b61b-57d5-8be6-96dc868fced6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31e24d23-144c-5f4c-83da-8b52dfbbc484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371882} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb25176c-9969-5abb-b583-813ca366126d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371880}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbbbf3ae-4ecb-5df7-bf03-c3a7be26b224" :question/math-academy-id "q-160852" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\ny=3x-1,\\,y=3x-3,\\,x=3y,\\,x=3y+2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n3x-y=1,\\,3x-y=3,\\,x-3y=0,\\,x-3y=2\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=3x-y,\\,v=x-3y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3x-y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-y=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & 3x-y=3\\,⟹\\,u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x-3y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x-3y=0\\,⟹\\,v=0 \\\\  & x-3y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a873a6bdd07f942d9c7e729e55354b6414259a2f38b52e7faf1ad0befa2c827c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-9+1 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{8}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccecda64-e614-576a-95ef-e05124675fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371888} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dbc0ee-bd56-5d09-82ae-0a50d663a617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371889} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1c272c-a26e-56c4-b420-6c0e9237be97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371890} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9488a7-26bc-5807-abd0-f5550f336827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371891} {:answer/id #uuid "49a9bf82-6b9b-5af5-bd83-8f2fc3e179b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e1c272c-a26e-56c4-b420-6c0e9237be97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371890} :answer-field/id #uuid "f732fda0-f223-5e8e-9cd7-3bad366f7805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371887}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1533ae23-2a8c-5746-8cc6-278953144598" :question/math-academy-id "q-160857" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n3y=4x+1,\\,3y=4x-2,\\,3x=2y+1,\\,3x=2y+3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n4x-3y=-1,\\,4x-3y=2,\\,3x-2y=1,\\,3x-2y=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=4x-3y,\\,v=3x-2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=4x-3y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4x-3y=-1\\,⟹\\,u=-1 \\\\  & 4x-3y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3x-2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 3x-2y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/5d/5dc4be989300dca3e52b7ff1dfb6a21c60409976fe55d42f5ae530f033725a64.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-8+9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "401d1340-9f46-59c1-abfc-5071d62dc570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371976} {:answer/id #uuid "3204bc80-8ab3-5312-ac12-252b1d788312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371977} {:answer/id #uuid "545f9367-80fc-527f-b725-9577794d6914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371978} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8e3cd4-7080-5e0f-b3c0-62f9a1c245aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371979} {:answer/id #uuid "2897d53d-b8f5-5abb-a72a-895d82c614bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2897d53d-b8f5-5abb-a72a-895d82c614bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371980} :answer-field/id #uuid "f77b7f7b-0f4d-55ec-986c-8391703e056e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371975}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2acf3d8-4862-5fab-a9f5-360ef711c3dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161869" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x},\\,x={y}^{2},\\,x=3{y}^{2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=1,\\,xy=4,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2d98eb2de472226d010fb633478f3662754679c2256e3fd3d06600a6599bc7f5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Mapping an Area to a Rectangular Region: Transformations Not Given"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128838 :question/id #uuid "eec994b4-82ac-568c-b493-dc9a21102c2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-11156" :question/problem "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/7f/7fc9eb28594744f04c1934d10b7e9751c9f9855897f4cc4da05b8a5e82a93f3d.svg)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](images/5e/5e056d63a91b0f4ae7dcb1c076764b862b0321b47c6e586cc91acd9e01dd36c8.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/37/370aba8042cc3dbd6c5c9dc15751e50dd8fe7be36d0cb220966da075bf9e2e43.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](images/1c/1ca433e68fd2a57e9bd9666b76c0577fd84621eededde3a50d03a49723b21739.svg) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab643519-92a4-5ccf-b826-fb2a68b36534" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129507 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "683571f2-07f3-587b-82c8-c91f90742301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276409} {:answer/id #uuid "875f9442-0384-5276-af8d-5acd1c256c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276697} {:answer/id #uuid "8db57813-6b8a-52b7-ac15-2e606c882c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276762} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3728d1-c1cf-5eef-a87d-a43db22f1937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277016} {:answer/id #uuid "cae52c74-ecff-52ad-a806-db5d5566d474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cae52c74-ecff-52ad-a806-db5d5566d474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277280} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a2c95f-16e9-55cb-83b5-4438e90426c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277874}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba3c81ec-9070-50b3-af3a-82e4e50bb35c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160380" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$ and $y(u,v)=3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-(-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =6u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,2)}=6(1)=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef5e4bf-4d3f-5f5c-ac33-42ad4c6e3790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275606} {:answer/id #uuid "845f2f7c-94cc-5110-b14d-4efb54d153b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276664} {:answer/id #uuid "899c753d-d349-5813-ae0c-d11e4c3c1c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276720} {:answer/id #uuid "d720bef8-4451-5b2f-95c9-1413643ea08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277371} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f59278-293f-5cf8-80d1-7875f8166412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "845f2f7c-94cc-5110-b14d-4efb54d153b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276664} :answer-field/id #uuid "482d9065-f53f-5f23-9e90-27007d015ddc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277839}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa6e908a-36ba-5f5c-b35a-6753290d1954" :question/math-academy-id "q-160394" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {e}^{u-v}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={e}^{u-v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ {e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (-{e}^{u-v})-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot {e}^{u-v} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2u}-{e}^{2u} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,-5)}=-2{e}^{2(0)}=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27184dd5-9306-5151-ba71-71c0184555b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275833} {:answer/id #uuid "921684d8-1a85-53da-b8a7-23bd356d4d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276799} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b6ecd9-7efa-5fbf-99f4-87cf3cf2fc2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276980} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4d180-9148-56f8-af89-c87393729376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277007} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4ef6cf-c02f-531a-934f-84c5afac9290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "921684d8-1a85-53da-b8a7-23bd356d4d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276799} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f823688-c59b-5a64-9e51-9b7c368087e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b1f8f7b-fd82-5c84-a276-7fa2fd9b071a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160383" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ \\frac{v}{u}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\frac{v}{u}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ -\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot \\frac{1}{u}\\text{ }-u\\cdot (-\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2v}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(3)}{-2}=-3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b2ac211-6ede-500a-9363-0c90ed1326ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275729} {:answer/id #uuid "241de261-1031-55ca-a058-00911568c512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275796} {:answer/id #uuid "54f35b13-d77e-5a66-b2b8-4f7e13bf0284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276233} {:answer/id #uuid "b3da8c48-0f97-556c-90bd-47b71461d13c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277086} {:answer/id #uuid "c49fcad0-5cc2-5449-8be5-a58c6c177d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b2ac211-6ede-500a-9363-0c90ed1326ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275729} :answer-field/id #uuid "795a7cef-1005-511a-9200-2ed1ac91ad82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2443aa6d-e746-5170-93b3-e30684a9a33b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160402" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u-v}$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(uv).$  \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u-v}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{v})-(-{e}^{u-v})\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{v}+\\frac{{e}^{u-v}}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}+\\frac{{e}^{(1)-(1)}}{(1)}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a030248-80e5-5392-828d-2bf5b0f34c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275988} {:answer/id #uuid "46441063-cd17-57f8-93da-47999b8389f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276099} {:answer/id #uuid "4d076db5-f0b8-5a55-b9f9-a751427c3ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276158} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6588ee-c55c-5f42-9ca1-f5f8f0e473ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276758} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0b5777-e3d4-5733-95a0-9024964404d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a030248-80e5-5392-828d-2bf5b0f34c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275988} :answer-field/id #uuid "ade1226d-3be4-5422-9ead-d085939320b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278025}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "861c1236-2662-5b7d-b150-03b6774d0f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-160427" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ {u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-2v\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-4uv-4uv \\\\  & =-8uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,1)}=-8(-2)(1)=16.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7338df5d-9ede-525b-86f7-9575217f0621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276521} {:answer/id #uuid "9194b3db-7eb6-5b12-82d1-c39ced725329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276796} {:answer/id #uuid "9f339d05-8ec4-52fc-929d-9e259b65d946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276908} {:answer/id #uuid "ae707e98-98f8-52a3-8963-4e1ec7a26492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277042} {:answer/id #uuid "e115090f-5258-55ab-a7de-95ef1c7a3b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7338df5d-9ede-525b-86f7-9575217f0621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276521} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc930da6-f008-59fd-875c-d797cf29d182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19415143-780a-5182-8164-c1af6838225c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160428" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\frac{{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}}{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}u & -v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =u\\cdot u-(-v)\\cdot v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e0e11f-ca64-573d-aa4a-39c0085748ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275562} {:answer/id #uuid "2571ec42-1410-5512-abb4-5d9f33e67cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275817} {:answer/id #uuid "6056f233-baaf-59fc-ab26-47501271c259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276339} {:answer/id #uuid "e97346e5-01ac-55ce-b840-edd902ee3c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277517} {:answer/id #uuid "fa81b135-dfdc-52eb-85bc-1dd61c22d3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e97346e5-01ac-55ce-b840-edd902ee3c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277517} :answer-field/id #uuid "946d36e5-48fa-5671-b5c2-6b9df0953fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277988}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "876a5d15-9cbd-5150-8246-c71dbabaa45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160431" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2}+uv \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={v}^{2}+uv$ and $y(u,v)=v-u.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v+u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & 2v+u \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-(2v+u)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =v+2v+u \\\\  & =3v+u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(8,-2)}=3(-2)+8=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201b5adb-fe9e-56a4-a103-cfa7fd40fca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275775} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7b2609-013a-5fdd-ad02-4411dd975fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276441} {:answer/id #uuid "7c015757-c6cd-5661-8772-840f2ddff2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276607} {:answer/id #uuid "803b5ba9-187d-5e4c-a266-d33068d508bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276640} {:answer/id #uuid "af79779a-7d08-51aa-a7a1-413703ca604d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af79779a-7d08-51aa-a7a1-413703ca604d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277048} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed0e005f-2ede-5db6-90a3-c44e813e721d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f68c62f-9f11-5647-83b4-1238f13388b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-160440" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡v-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{6},0)}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc0ac04-220f-5368-899c-f4b853d6caf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369875} {:answer/id #uuid "f515d3db-880a-5d23-99f0-9cb65e633ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369876} {:answer/id #uuid "57254211-1cc3-5a7f-87e1-0c345d6ba0b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369877} {:answer/id #uuid "0ab4227a-a1c7-56de-9a4e-de04cc753871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369878} {:answer/id #uuid "47ca5fb3-37c5-5519-9b75-0484cfdf1808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dc0ac04-220f-5368-899c-f4b853d6caf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369875} :answer-field/id #uuid "c478e331-2d1a-585a-9599-198d7061ae49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369874}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91c905cb-29ef-5cbc-a791-764e51ba7eed" :question/math-academy-id "q-160445" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b616ca6f-9ed2-50cf-882d-c8ff1503540f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383517} {:answer/id #uuid "e8495e34-5713-5977-90f2-f1d23c192117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383518} {:answer/id #uuid "f3941799-b16e-5c96-afab-c5161cad125c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383519} {:answer/id #uuid "8235a654-792e-5e9c-921e-555040b5595a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383520} {:answer/id #uuid "2326b5cf-7dc7-542b-9226-1befd327ddd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b616ca6f-9ed2-50cf-882d-c8ff1503540f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383517} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeaaf6e0-abe7-5c25-953e-f15cf6c1b87a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7031c6b-f8db-5a21-9b28-fac40f0a2c66" :question/math-academy-id "q-160434" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ and $y(u,v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v))-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v))\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is \n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e0d4b0b-8093-5290-b6b4-484e2a3c019b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417350} {:answer/id #uuid "3f52c558-a8f4-5f12-84e4-136a7b9663ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417351} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3faf64-35fa-5814-9c5b-2c546aef7f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417352} {:answer/id #uuid "c802b759-c3a3-5234-a525-52e3dcf0d733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417353} {:answer/id #uuid "df929076-a9d7-5c37-b534-2a48f5c0f2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c802b759-c3a3-5234-a525-52e3dcf0d733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417353} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6520f4c-cce8-5e56-adce-0289e4c3517a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417355}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2756cd36-f06f-563c-8e51-e0b858d03f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-160387" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ u+2{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where\n$[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+2{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =4v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 4v-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24uv-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,1)}=24(1)(1)-2=22.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Jacobian Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129267 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128844 :question/id #uuid "c6495ab5-3756-50e6-8977-7ec19fba4466" :question/math-academy-id "e-11470" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2 + y^2 = 4$ for $y \\geq 0$ between the surfaces $z =0$ and $z = 10 - x.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R y \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9a/9ad0d507a5440cfce9595e9054457fd733ae412d9a57b0a85238b4773ff0a0c7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10-x = 10 - r\\cos\\theta.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi, \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 10-r\\cos\\theta \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3688ce9-ceff-524b-ac44-0224526c861b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2585088-05d7-5d58-84a4-aa6edca772f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6 + r sin ⁡ θ}r 2 z sin ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126597} {:answer/id #uuid "e8020774-1748-5d5e-80a9-f8d1cd9f368e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π / 2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6 + r sin ⁡ θ}r 2 z cos ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126598} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c9c242-6056-5479-8012-68e49f23d93c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π / 2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6 + r cos ⁡ θ}r 2 z cos ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126599} {:answer/id #uuid "16547f0c-d132-55d4-87de-591c39f35e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π / 2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6 + r sin ⁡ θ}r z cos ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126600} {:answer/id #uuid "76e8994a-e005-55e5-af7a-c1cdad465df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6 + r cos ⁡ θ}r z sin ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126601}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5659b1e7-edd6-5858-a187-e1aacd269a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126596}] :question/id #uuid "1a6db462-1cdd-5328-ac3f-82ce0a5c5a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-161520" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 25$ for $x \\ge 0$ between the surfaces $z = 0$ and $z = 6 + y$.\nExpress the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)xzdV$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94aadad7-3353-5170-a5a1-d94e0dd91e72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10 - r cos ⁡ θ}r zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127916} {:answer/id #uuid "a25dfbc8-54b3-5802-b238-d346d2326ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10}r 2 zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127917} {:answer/id #uuid "dd096352-52ff-520a-9256-99c4b17974ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10 - r cos ⁡ θ}r 2 zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127918} {:answer/id #uuid "0a850283-aa76-5b31-a337-1ad320968c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{9}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10 - r sin ⁡ θ}r 2 zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127919} {:answer/id #uuid "494dcffc-925c-5deb-be37-3c33e9b46f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{9}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{10}r 2 zdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127920}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ef5d7669-0b63-5970-8efc-622f5c6b1a62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127915} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7534b4b-0be3-57d1-9602-f5788953a87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10}{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368536} {:answer/id #uuid "83bc9502-bd0e-55c2-88f7-c99dfeb46a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368537} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa867b2-61b8-564d-bf6d-b7e1d2f94971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368538} {:answer/id #uuid "35e7552c-f179-58ed-93c4-67028d32cb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368539} {:answer/id #uuid "eb28f31a-ad06-547b-a0a7-98b1ef8b5e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{1}^{10}{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fa867b2-61b8-564d-bf6d-b7e1d2f94971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368538} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1676e97-4bf4-5377-a977-58856345b080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368535}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d9d79e2-4f89-52ac-a84b-fb5b127d47eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-39481" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ between the surfaces $z=1$ and $z=10-x.$\nExpress the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10-x=10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,1\\le z\\le 10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}z\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}z\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccd9d0aa-3e58-51b4-aa65-f2fab94c1045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{r}r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367661} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3616c8-9a5d-54a6-8d6b-c0ccc09a58e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367662} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc39972-cdfa-5f06-8bdd-ea431268d777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367663} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa4178f-c535-5a4a-8c93-a33931081fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{r}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367664} {:answer/id #uuid "937fccb4-3f00-5741-b712-a541b3052af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "937fccb4-3f00-5741-b712-a541b3052af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367665} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2ef7d1-0525-5600-9694-dd8bdf3b7386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367660}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea39193a-bd12-54e0-b687-ee3f9d3c2eec" :question/math-academy-id "q-161605" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=1.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/f4/f4c6f3acefa5d89fd5064de469952da3d68d379559bb4a6a4990a2ea24e4f861.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{z}^{2}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}r{z}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6865fde3-5faa-5c6c-9848-626d31d1faf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-r}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367668} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f89762-3cbb-55f2-ae7d-80400ebd3d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-r}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367669} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf3d1ed-b7ce-54e2-b809-9211a789e705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367670} {:answer/id #uuid "ed644672-0769-5dd7-ae28-d54203b97a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367671} {:answer/id #uuid "17de9ee2-1a3d-54e2-adb3-9772d2ea22b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddf3d1ed-b7ce-54e2-b809-9211a789e705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367670} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e263cd-6a7c-550d-852b-30e38d3c9ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367667}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0602a248-c988-5793-8ea6-c87cceac7390" :question/math-academy-id "q-161607" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/36/36839d1fbde65e86df7335d40374fe72a5ff43ed92b89405327406a62dc8763c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{1+z}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac64e85f-c75e-5695-8748-bba429dfbf1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367675} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee3a9f1-edc8-5a06-9c64-03a304c45a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367676} {:answer/id #uuid "01eebf3e-1158-5e20-80e4-ac97089def3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367677} {:answer/id #uuid "e4afce7b-06df-5b92-8141-ceddfc731240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367678} {:answer/id #uuid "21cd2e06-5c99-57e5-a1a5-95c6355405e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4afce7b-06df-5b92-8141-ceddfc731240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367678} :answer-field/id #uuid "b021fe61-7dff-56cd-841b-a803cdfcc585" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367674}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5fb33f0-d9af-50dd-ae91-99a85b0b4ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161611" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ for $x\\le 0,$ between the surfaces $z=x$ and $z={y}^{2}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/16/16b7c2f69bb20df2faafabf10368776cb69b608bb61eb8432495ae5f146e7100.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={y}^{2}={r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}r({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179016a8-0967-5aa2-8749-987e9232c473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367682} {:answer/id #uuid "ed409635-e038-5761-8147-f34be88d7eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367683} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0451c2-89db-5c8c-b802-ada9b3135d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367684} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8f6a4e-1068-54f9-bfa2-44dea6b6c19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367685} {:answer/id #uuid "3d42aec9-1d36-56e8-8bbb-4d0cd2b495d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8f6a4e-1068-54f9-bfa2-44dea6b6c19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367685} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e8df57e-c904-5c32-8f54-f331ca9fbb46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367681}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93fe857d-875f-5d05-8b0e-e6f2a6566317" :question/math-academy-id "q-161612" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ for $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=-1$ and $z=y+2.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/7d/7d3ef0b0a9a2f1c9ab60b4ed09f8a0318c57f74236e404baf181e0197ef66002.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=y+2=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-1\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}z((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}rz({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{3}z({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}{\\int }_{-1}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa8d886-4e3c-5e48-b553-b40bcf5fb8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427869} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff27779-26b4-5f50-86c1-895b48b634f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-r}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427872} {:answer/id #uuid "845094cf-59d5-5624-ae32-03926b0d10f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427877} {:answer/id #uuid "880a8f8a-f3c5-58bb-8d3c-3a0e61a860b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-r}}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427878} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf0414c-80b1-5d90-b7d4-18b04ab2c214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fa8d886-4e3c-5e48-b553-b40bcf5fb8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427869} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d846f8-2c72-5e60-9280-2f6e5faaefd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427900}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29c4348e-6b58-58ee-9f24-8f11cdb0cbe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161613" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ for $x\\le 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/8d/8dc4ea0ad2bb776fb31132e1ff8d97efdd2c166dc23ffbc8641f8d7034b8d487.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48587302-f52a-53a7-9f31-47825d8a1075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r}^{3}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427870} {:answer/id #uuid "841fab14-6f1b-500a-ab2e-cd3ad15098fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427876} {:answer/id #uuid "90b8d8fe-ee7b-5218-86d0-ad24858cf4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{{r}^{2}}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427881} {:answer/id #uuid "a75b7c7c-ec28-5349-b0da-7fdd1b563361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{{r}^{2}}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427882} {:answer/id #uuid "b90b5f49-c248-5418-886a-73d93a5226bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{r}^{3}r\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "841fab14-6f1b-500a-ab2e-cd3ad15098fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427876} :answer-field/id #uuid "587e6da7-3775-5bfa-a95f-b626a3ad5c01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427897}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34d0810f-9c28-578b-861d-9088f229fffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161604" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ between the surfaces $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $z=3.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,r\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{r}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Cylindrical Solid"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129268 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128845 :question/id #uuid "043cc93d-1eae-5640-a109-9acbe7a34f92" :question/math-academy-id "e-11471" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\, : \\, (x-1)^2 + y^2 \\leq 1, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-y \\right\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta, z) \\:  r \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\, \\mathrm{d}r \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a5/a551e379767395a2422f690a66fc38defab64cff03c752c0c9a5a0748561d2f7.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z = -2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z = 4 - y = 4 - r\\sin{\\theta}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-1)^2 + y^2 = 1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r = 2\\cos{\\theta}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta,z) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq r \\leq 2\\cos{\\theta}, \\: -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: -2 \\leq z \\leq 4-r\\sin{\\theta} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{4-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aea71b56-dd09-564e-a2bf-5606233e2819" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8677d1f9-393e-54ba-86b0-46dcf9a4cbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{π}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{-6 sin ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 + r 2}r 3 cos ⁡ θ sin ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126603} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8d8993-e95e-5762-b88f-6db309b818d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{π}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 + r}r 3 cos ⁡ θ sin ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126604} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa7b7c9-1d88-53f2-9f82-f9e192924e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{π}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6 cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 + r 2}r 3 cos ⁡ θ sin ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126605} {:answer/id #uuid "7276902e-fc5c-5ff0-8c83-e43a7bab8048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{π}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 + r}r 2 cos ⁡ θ sin ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126606} {:answer/id #uuid "779e048b-2b47-573b-8edc-2defad579596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{π}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{-6 sin ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 + r 2}r 2 cos ⁡ θ sin ⁡ θdzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126607}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b7d66d94-89fa-5367-8f41-89e27a901a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126602}] :question/id #uuid "0593d504-dbf8-59c9-9282-2775ebdf4b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-161521" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): x^{2} + (y + 3)^{2} \\le 9, 0 \\le z \\le 2 + x^{2} + y^{2}}$,\nthen $∭_(R)xydV =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81187673-7ac0-5e58-b248-69eeca227853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 2dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126615} {:answer/id #uuid "9e372ca8-c288-5b6f-af34-3b37cdf41d36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 2dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126616} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb260c0-24e5-580f-9506-c5c76f158580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{-cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 3dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126617} {:answer/id #uuid "05fd1905-8d14-5074-89d3-a42787783e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 cos ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 3dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126618} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca051cd-833c-5347-aa63-bcbbf6080651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{-2 sin ⁡ θ}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r 3dzdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126619}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5de2e9ed-6a8e-5e65-b0f4-c004ab1d9bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126614}] :question/id #uuid "cc7985de-f7b8-5b62-b788-d731ea9f3ce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161523" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1, y \\ge 0, 1 \\le z \\le 2}$,\nthen $∭_(R)x^{2} + y^{2}dV =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b265b7cc-feb9-5f37-9252-3a78cad0bd5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367724} {:answer/id #uuid "8580f07f-10cc-5678-a359-0d89f29a5582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367725} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8af12c-fb84-5a5a-a7a2-521fb178be84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367726} {:answer/id #uuid "54950957-60fb-56ff-96f5-e10b21a3d9ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367727} {:answer/id #uuid "dff4f68f-af71-5932-8072-ae940cffd5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dff4f68f-af71-5932-8072-ae940cffd5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367728} :answer-field/id #uuid "8751e409-d7fb-5aec-8818-db14c330ee72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f13fe1ec-6a87-5577-82ff-9825a15656c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161630" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,-8\\le z\\le x+2},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/3c/3c03dde21942c60de527ace896bb51ad8070781126d72f5be5e2f6069700736d.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(-3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-8,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x+2=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,-8\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-8}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367729 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b100d2-4636-55d3-9270-cc48d2b6fc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367731} {:answer/id #uuid "9a29a4a1-8401-5c26-985a-c9e53ed3d39d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367732} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe69c16-aea5-5c84-b3e2-6e91f9235a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367733} {:answer/id #uuid "6d67683a-c100-5cd2-85f2-e42c45195bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367734} {:answer/id #uuid "30a2338f-aa3a-5e0e-a5d0-96cd484338d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19b100d2-4636-55d3-9270-cc48d2b6fc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367731} :answer-field/id #uuid "29402b40-ddfd-5d71-9239-ec1aa69d5ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367730}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d873cc92-450d-547b-9f0c-590b7060a8cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161636" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 6},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/cc/ccc5cab37aed89c0d701ce0195414796211ddc73a498eaaa1ff145a45c42e9fd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(3,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =3 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le z\\le 6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{6}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07316687-6f83-5fed-b3ec-6bddf779b76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427865} {:answer/id #uuid "8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427880} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6ce7d9-8ba5-51ed-8961-057d4fc31ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427884} {:answer/id #uuid "b00512c6-580b-5ace-be32-2aca9c459007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427885} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c92edf-23c0-5c0c-b7bb-0f570aaf2732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a070aa5-47af-5201-85a2-9449ca643060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427880} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4c28d8-7673-5151-abc1-64dbe75c6bbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427898}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f86783a2-30c3-5d18-9eb0-dc2efdbbae6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161633" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,x\\le 0,\\,-2\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/83/831de8cf1d9833f8674a7e4cb69d01098970eefb9cf576c0537fa7be471c8c2c.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{16-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,-2\\le z\\le \\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-2}^{\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6caf10ce-b939-51d0-8ac6-b9727918700b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427874} {:answer/id #uuid "88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427879} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5a0734-d3ec-5220-8c59-ae2d980b4839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427883} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e25a86-fc0b-500c-9ea6-617a19745775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427888} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e27cb0-5d40-54cb-b1f4-f82c347f9fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88813988-bed1-58d5-9361-f7b9c3b921af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427879} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae592001-7c80-5683-9d64-6ebfa88e4b3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427899}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa2c3789-4a9d-5deb-a378-eba9cd152cf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161637" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,-5\\le z\\le x\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b6/b66b8db18b2f5773addc2d00c02f2f15f4a7ead53323418e63bd50457f74466b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-5\\le z\\le r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1}\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{-5}^{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Cylindrical Solid"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129269 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128846 :question/id #uuid "b22d04e0-8c6a-507a-9d76-ab05adb32b10" :question/math-academy-id "e-11517" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n![](images/f8/f82cf760dee34626c290e29420fbaddb4ef254180087d05bb6fb883f2dc32e75.svg)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](images/1c/1c2567cd1c2cf28ed17b6dc445025cb60061dc7d7d6c78c960be77f8866ef4a7.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad \nv(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad\nw(x,y,z) = yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x|\\begin{aligned}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x(xy-0) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y & =2\\cdot x\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot v \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "408e29c9-6522-5467-9ab8-def9fe8cfb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324490} {:answer/id #uuid "69224e59-e1c5-54cd-8748-b2d597b5cd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{w}{u}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324848} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1e869c-a3b7-584b-8e0f-e99423947eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325004} {:answer/id #uuid "e98f16dc-8225-5762-aa80-dca5e80b6645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326027} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5ec600-18ee-54e3-bb28-7c22c6783223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e98f16dc-8225-5762-aa80-dca5e80b6645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326027} :answer-field/id #uuid "65802821-8118-5ece-8435-a69816ad88f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326394}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f85af4c0-4520-5614-952b-cb9d6d06c6c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39300" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}+z \\\\ {y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0$ and $y>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{y}+z,\\,z(x,y,z)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}} & =(\\frac{1}{x})^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{w} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a3dfa61-4983-5101-a590-b7cae3069606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324062} {:answer/id #uuid "5975b6e2-4d63-5522-bc15-85311b4304c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324714} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d3c1d8-1ca4-5893-ae40-8a4995898733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325383} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fd3b47-8f20-518a-a996-2dbd4fa2edc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325679} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb623d2-5638-5465-9671-82a386df23f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5fd3b47-8f20-518a-a996-2dbd4fa2edc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325679} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d44cd8a-7ab2-5c59-a740-4c19610ad44c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326377}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ad6bc33-7c9a-5d03-8204-18b81f486eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-39669" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=-x+3y-2z,\\,w=3y+4z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(12+6)-1(-4-0) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d889e7-c824-5206-ae1f-be2ca84ac201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278809} {:answer/id #uuid "54eae97a-126b-5169-968a-945a94267a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279365} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2bc2ed-1ff1-5cb1-beed-8d5a3ea43e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280124} {:answer/id #uuid "b815cee2-db42-5d6f-beef-5073c80b1726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280211} {:answer/id #uuid "feaf148b-7dd0-5012-95f0-0e1627a40f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54eae97a-126b-5169-968a-945a94267a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279365} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2c215b-9be4-534d-b695-b69d79a8eec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280877}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "867c4c3a-9ff3-5bda-af78-c2d51483fcb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161798" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=z,\\,w=4y-2{z}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b82b5c-a4bc-5662-ada6-dda7518ac8c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278655} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc85f22-4a58-5c43-9914-7b18675f3668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278731} {:answer/id #uuid "413c7a3a-e6cb-5e3d-b351-58d589d29f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279171} {:answer/id #uuid "7cce492a-e940-5ea6-a555-ee7f7af4f9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279706} {:answer/id #uuid "ffff14cf-d01e-51fb-a239-eecaf7d789e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fc85f22-4a58-5c43-9914-7b18675f3668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278731} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd07e6bc-736c-579e-90c2-ab826af79391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281217}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9050d05-b087-5029-bf1c-8747b808c301" :question/math-academy-id "q-161800" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=z-y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=-3x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =-3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0+3)+1(0-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c2caa68-f00a-5537-8bd9-f6cb76c877ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279273} {:answer/id #uuid "600dfa31-4e37-5a16-ba48-c5ca10f9fbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279473} {:answer/id #uuid "b204a652-49ca-5415-ba78-58814749d96e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280165} {:answer/id #uuid "c706e56c-9384-5437-a653-797286bbede3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280336} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5dc6c7-df6e-5fdb-b0fa-59f45de8fd17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa5dc6c7-df6e-5fdb-b0fa-59f45de8fd17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280789} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d4e8c1-df31-56ff-9384-be31853aa6b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280891}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f0a4eb6-83e4-58b7-81d7-e22ca020da04" :question/math-academy-id "q-161801" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=y-3z,\\,v=x+2z,\\,w=3x-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(0-6)-3(-1-0) \\\\  & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6cc79b-3207-598f-8d70-80b25ee5e718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278846} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d99844-e42f-56e8-86a6-5d5458f5fa76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280045} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c82ac3-d2d1-560d-a1c4-451dfa40fa91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280052} {:answer/id #uuid "cc93804e-fbe9-508c-968d-dea27a808ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280388} {:answer/id #uuid "f373368b-6b80-5e55-91f1-61c0a4bf9baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f373368b-6b80-5e55-91f1-61c0a4bf9baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280720} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6d4ed4-6f38-58ef-90e7-56012b55823a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78cdb0a8-90d4-5ce3-9ce4-dfaf056d437e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161802" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=3x+yz,\\,v=z,\\,w=-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & z & y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(0+1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b44cea0-ad33-5976-923f-1f474b30b292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{u+v+w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278975} {:answer/id #uuid "460aadea-67c4-543b-8250-5f0974161a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v+w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279217} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4a44db-5da9-5c22-8623-1c9463ab6122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279447} {:answer/id #uuid "b93d3489-33b8-59f0-b4e7-ed465bfeb6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u+v+w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280221} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb87757-2b77-5b42-9577-c7fa19032c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebb87757-2b77-5b42-9577-c7fa19032c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280664} :answer-field/id #uuid "00162f26-ff18-503b-9399-9d89f9b3560f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280834}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "256171d6-afaa-51cd-ae04-b8a4f7e24f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-161804" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} \\\\ {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)={e}^{x},\\,v(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y},\\,w(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & ={e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{2x+2y+z}-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x+2y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x+2y+z} & ={e}^{x+(x+y)+(x+y+z)} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x+y+z} \\\\  & =uvw\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d44b6cc-3a93-5117-a27f-6f280b9b11c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278862} {:answer/id #uuid "47b2751a-712d-5998-bd6d-e5e60e8c5b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{v+w}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279233} {:answer/id #uuid "7a98c4b3-ed46-5cfd-8a6c-e55f7a475d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(w-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279683} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa88b3c-e8a4-5165-97f7-0e1e8c3eec3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279962} {:answer/id #uuid "f792ff52-2762-5037-adc2-ea50c01f1006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d44b6cc-3a93-5117-a27f-6f280b9b11c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278862} :answer-field/id #uuid "db389b2e-8734-58ef-b276-82b4a342763c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281214}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21d2a60a-f1f7-5e79-815f-2e9babe829a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161805" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ z-y \\\\ xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=x,\\,v(x,y,z)=z-y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ y & x & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ x & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-x) \\\\  & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21988933-2fdd-5343-979b-14ba2f6e68d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278894} {:answer/id #uuid "413d8651-c816-595c-bfd1-f51fa29900be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279172} {:answer/id #uuid "4e964f3b-c23d-5355-8277-f886ef034e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279306} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc53c83-70d3-5bd8-adc4-31458e829c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4uvw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279694} {:answer/id #uuid "8888de73-401c-58c0-8c74-d084f5008000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "413d8651-c816-595c-bfd1-f51fa29900be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279172} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb87408-7532-5806-bc11-5c1c98d338e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281257}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bd3e29c-50b2-5640-b3fa-d0adf89e1fac" :question/math-academy-id "q-161806" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=xy,\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y|\\begin{aligned}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{aligned}|-x|\\begin{aligned}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y(xz-0)-x(0-yz) \\\\  & =xyz+xyz \\\\  & =2xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz & =2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0626020c-ffd0-5d52-be60-af389fc83a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(uvw)^{1/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278648} {:answer/id #uuid "57bde7fd-ba02-508b-9c77-edf62367e8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279398} {:answer/id #uuid "84467733-f429-5feb-8388-07e2d51f176e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(uvw)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279778} {:answer/id #uuid "af416bf2-0b93-5782-b6de-1f1c2d402fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uvw)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280143} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f31297-6e1d-54c7-be43-6fdd25ab29fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{(uvw)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57bde7fd-ba02-508b-9c77-edf62367e8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279398} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbffbbf-30e0-555b-b5e7-55199b3bfffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280913}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82aa7a5f-aede-5ec9-8984-8cdea4eb6f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-161807" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}y{z}^{2} \\\\ z{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2},\\,v(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2},\\,w(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={z}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2xz & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={y}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2xy & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & {z}^{2} & 2yz \\\\ 2xz & 0 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}|\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 0\\end{aligned}|+2yz|\\begin{aligned}2xz & 0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}(0-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+2yz(4{x}^{2}yz-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} & =9{({x}^{3}{y}^{3}{z}^{3})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9{(y{z}^{2}\\cdot z{x}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9(uvw)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba467583-3a17-5baa-8344-2ff452a1d080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424165} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7a65b8-5911-5995-890e-26eb26de2d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{vw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424166} {:answer/id #uuid "cda476b6-e024-5c13-a813-a0217e4446ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424167} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf34300-0330-5f0e-9e12-24a74c4b83db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2u}{v-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424170} {:answer/id #uuid "fa885cf6-9f29-528c-8aeb-86e960fc0083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{w}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba467583-3a17-5baa-8344-2ff452a1d080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424165} :answer-field/id #uuid "f602c505-c9b2-58a6-873a-3101dc02439a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424177}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bbf87425-cede-5e7e-870f-278bd2b11904" :question/math-academy-id "q-161808" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ y \\\\ xz\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{y},\\,v(x,y,z)=y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =z|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =z(0-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{z}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z}{y} & =-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0584ff70-04f9-53f4-b918-d40c95661794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424148} {:answer/id #uuid "274585a0-b8c0-5396-9ba1-f844d74ca493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424151} {:answer/id #uuid "2dad3fa0-8b4c-595d-a450-31167f619d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424152} {:answer/id #uuid "d83940ba-986b-5a11-adf3-d93e8cc8e5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424168} {:answer/id #uuid "f587a66a-7aeb-56f0-8880-52bcb09867b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83940ba-986b-5a11-adf3-d93e8cc8e5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9faf9fb-252c-510a-b14d-4675e0404650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424175}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa96e0b4-9142-5b9e-b173-68571a2c2de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161797" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=2y,\\,w=3z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(6-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129270 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128847 :question/id #uuid "b16845a2-5f66-524a-90ea-37f8a8186f8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-11518" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n![](images/22/22690753019e406703952ae0c2a79450764b5ff42c758a03b2875a9d428d9e78.svg)" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](images/be/be4c52a967cf6430f1d365ebb9b3a3a20bc852ac6422ed51e01dc5b39055c816.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w) =w-u^3, \\qquad \ny(u,v,w) = 2uv, \\qquad\nz(u,v,w) = u^2+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3{u}^{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 2u & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2v & 2u \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}(0-0)+1(2v-4{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} =0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2(v-2{u}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-2u^2 = 0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $v=2u^2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ef2524-c316-5f5c-bb17-9410d6df7855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u = 2v$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126656} {:answer/id #uuid "af21f9b2-947b-5b4b-9b83-b2324aabe6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u =-w^{2}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126657} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ef1406-0abe-5cfe-bc04-7ae04a77ecf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u = w$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126658} {:answer/id #uuid "e90dd075-4fb2-51bb-9367-44b42c15619d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw -$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126659} {:answer/id #uuid "0cffeeef-f100-51b5-ac9e-aa76adbf7807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u = 2w^{2}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126660}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4d271abc-b1d9-5284-9062-68834149c800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126655} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59ecd82e-c85b-5221-81f4-5aa437d23a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401290} {:answer/id #uuid "1680c5f5-1c51-52cd-a03a-ad30d3c07c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=2{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401291} {:answer/id #uuid "92c1139b-4ea6-5a6e-91ff-1cd1ee2688f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401292} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea440aa-4bbc-5828-a750-3ec0ce76a35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401293} {:answer/id #uuid "b1928d32-8a83-5bc0-a68f-3b8d2a17bd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea440aa-4bbc-5828-a750-3ec0ce76a35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401293} :answer-field/id #uuid "17caa721-6c88-5948-86f1-24aa0f77f9b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401289}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "685e989d-8721-5a8c-a4d8-0e44efe4bc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161819" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{w}^{2} \\\\ w{e}^{u} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-{w}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u},\\,z(u,v,w)=u-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 & -2w \\\\ w{e}^{u} & 0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2w|\\begin{aligned}w{e}^{u} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(0+{e}^{u})-2w(-w{e}^{u}-0) \\\\  & =2u{e}^{u}+2{w}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u) & =0 \\\\ {w}^{2}+u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${w}^{2}+u=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f2fafa-94fa-537a-9de2-cde9b153270f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402683} {:answer/id #uuid "06b7dea6-5d67-5db6-9562-564a467e6927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402684} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdabe0b-331f-59b0-be6a-e0b333cf7d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder ${w}^{2}=2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402685} {:answer/id #uuid "edca51bc-5f25-52b9-81ee-5e945aaea583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402686} {:answer/id #uuid "2daa5374-5e80-5f12-9e7f-d5612c496bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f2fafa-94fa-537a-9de2-cde9b153270f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402683} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec45c948-28d5-5ad6-a116-f2bf6a3a31b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402682}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab44c8de-700d-51cc-ab4a-3755f7926aef" :question/math-academy-id "q-39709" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}+v \\\\ {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-v,\\,y(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+v,\\,z(u,v,w)={e}^{w}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(2u+2u) \\\\  & =4u{e}^{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 4u{e}^{w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b1e167f-c36b-51bb-8f7d-1c5abfba9d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the cylinder ${v}^{2}+{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278842} {:answer/id #uuid "398d98c4-80d9-5695-9ad1-7904a7156b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279097} {:answer/id #uuid "50abbce6-3f16-512e-a087-34dc285e7cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279329} {:answer/id #uuid "cc12c5cd-4583-53a4-9f13-8cababa0d611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280382} {:answer/id #uuid "de43abc5-071a-5046-a899-84beee7c637b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50abbce6-3f16-512e-a087-34dc285e7cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279329} :answer-field/id #uuid "73950424-d736-5015-94bb-837d8bf513c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281040}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d47d2404-bd86-5706-ba43-4f015172617a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161809" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ u+v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=uv,\\,y(u,v,w)=u+v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-u=0$ defines the plane $v=u..$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "710d7d7d-97b4-592f-b30c-acf388d38859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279617} {:answer/id #uuid "76ca4acf-773c-501c-ba6e-25239ada0435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279651} {:answer/id #uuid "7b363e77-7f40-535f-a3a7-6bd580690682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279692} {:answer/id #uuid "88ce4141-06f6-5738-b4cf-0fb6b9a3a05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the planes $v=\\pm w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279813} {:answer/id #uuid "eaed37b4-3321-5a47-9faa-28666138fd1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88ce4141-06f6-5738-b4cf-0fb6b9a3a05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the planes $v=\\pm w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279813} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf9576c-0ce8-5723-bb46-cfba69aeb672" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281170}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f26bca76-37a5-5f68-8e4a-4d9215b02535" :question/math-academy-id "q-161812" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ {v}^{3}+{w}^{3} \\\\ v+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{3}+{w}^{3},\\,z(u,v,w)=v+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3{w}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3{v}^{2}-3{w}^{2}) \\\\  & =3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2}-{w}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}-{w}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting planes $v=w$ and $v=-w.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5c045a-d984-5c99-8fa3-0129ac115c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278721} {:answer/id #uuid "2d089752-d1ca-57d4-aaad-39bd60fd867f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278988} {:answer/id #uuid "768c6810-e6a9-5693-b6ba-88bee24c33f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279649} {:answer/id #uuid "9f89b797-8be9-5dae-9e56-f0b7e9d1b9f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280008} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5b203f-69ef-5d8b-b9f1-3986949f0305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "768c6810-e6a9-5693-b6ba-88bee24c33f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279649} :answer-field/id #uuid "55be6ad3-d866-57e2-8467-8385c57eb9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280977}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2d2dfd0-abce-5610-a53e-633420f43575" :question/math-academy-id "q-161815" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{u} \\\\ vw \\\\ u+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\sqrt{u},\\,y(u,v,w)=vw,\\,z(u,v,w)=u+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & w & v \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}|\\begin{aligned}w & v \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}(w-0)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}} & =0 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w=0$ defines the plane $w=0,$ i.e., $uv$-plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012e5e71-30c4-576f-9027-84673f0cec11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278593} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca3f8ee-ee06-5aa0-b1ee-b1a91215f89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278855} {:answer/id #uuid "aedf9c47-b56b-5e71-845a-05c0a9e74384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the cylinder ${v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280139} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e3d435-b0b0-5133-a7de-47ee24110c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280736} {:answer/id #uuid "f7242b75-d7a6-5b47-a2a4-a6f542bd4ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ca3f8ee-ee06-5aa0-b1ee-b1a91215f89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278855} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0414cad-2a72-50dc-8e82-0bf67299254f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ae1d4bd-a84e-5282-96ec-f8259badfc91" :question/math-academy-id "q-161817" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2u+v+3w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=v{e}^{w},\\,z(u,v,w)=2u+v+3w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3{e}^{w}-v{e}^{w}) \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(3-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{w}(3-v) & =0 \\\\ 3-v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-v=0$ defines the plane $v=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0360b3bf-0e11-5644-974a-fb91e4539e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278610} {:answer/id #uuid "40d23535-8e40-5b39-96da-dc0c5913b017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the planes $v=\\pm w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279167} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4e4c43-7095-567c-8087-ac111d00d4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279286} {:answer/id #uuid "a24f7a5b-f9c2-51ab-bfe8-2b6ef54faa12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$ plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280034} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ca341c-0372-5300-8b3a-24814e46f956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ca341c-0372-5300-8b3a-24814e46f956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280285} :answer-field/id #uuid "0966bd13-e522-5f74-88dd-66c421ecae33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5828c68b-3870-5e91-8f73-8351ac68b7ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-161818" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+3w \\\\ v+2w \\\\ {v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)={v}^{2}-u.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4v)+3(0+1) \\\\  & =3-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3-4v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-4v=0$ defines the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b9f18b-ec2f-51d5-ae14-92461f9907ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $wv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376126} {:answer/id #uuid "5bacd98b-62c2-5e98-a210-80793aec69cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376127} {:answer/id #uuid "13bfe028-f756-50dc-a99d-e4bd7e879772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376128} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad07193-94a4-5bd2-b02f-54c7365c5656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=-2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376129} {:answer/id #uuid "69b5ad50-abba-5674-a016-be9139fe02be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13bfe028-f756-50dc-a99d-e4bd7e879772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376128} :answer-field/id #uuid "88cb516a-a7eb-5761-97af-05385415c8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376125}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a710b40d-30b7-5142-a877-a2aee690f184" :question/math-academy-id "q-161813" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ {v}^{2}+w \\\\ vw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\frac{1}{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2}+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}|\\begin{aligned}2v & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w) & =0 \\\\ w-2{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w-2{v}^{2}=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f14d31-90ea-5326-97dc-45022d0835ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u+v-2w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401277} {:answer/id #uuid "b740b568-485c-5311-a423-33ea2f3eeffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the elliptic cylinder ${u}^{2}+2{w}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401278} {:answer/id #uuid "a6795027-5482-5bff-8214-2dd2cc097fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401279} {:answer/id #uuid "5adbc1f1-362c-537c-bb50-2cdecc09b123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$ plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401280} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a89ced-7070-57be-88cf-4d4b894bd3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3a89ced-7070-57be-88cf-4d4b894bd3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401281} :answer-field/id #uuid "65676948-b2fa-585d-8619-ca89f50c6f34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401276}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8752c18-2285-5bd2-a3b2-ba4d0420bd04" :question/math-academy-id "q-161814" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+2w \\\\ v-u \\\\ uw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=uw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ w & 0 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ w & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(u-0)+2(0-w) \\\\  & =u-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ u-2w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u-2w=0$ defines the plane $u=2w.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76c85a4-c631-5c36-941d-c14613479d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401284} {:answer/id #uuid "10a3627e-14c0-553d-8005-b8e65337d0b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=-2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401285} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ffd8d2-f852-57f7-9970-8454309f0281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $2u+v+w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401286} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fe9b5d-4fbe-56d7-9627-c5c1df64a715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=-2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401287} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cbb05e-806b-56aa-8136-04b088a6dc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b76c85a4-c631-5c36-941d-c14613479d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401284} :answer-field/id #uuid "175d9200-8100-5e54-b984-b7451df1967c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc380b3f-d92e-5c45-8651-fac240787b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-161816" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cfa4e4-4978-5d07-b498-b67163d323ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406760} {:answer/id #uuid "47f0e73b-3fcc-5922-ac4c-5cbbb15dc078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406762} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd27081-6df8-5fcc-87e8-0b763e12dd24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406763} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd4d236-b060-5654-b754-6a3374d3448b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406766} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaabaf5-f0e8-54a9-9f5a-d6c4751feaf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06cfa4e4-4978-5d07-b498-b67163d323ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406760} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a97003-66fd-5b44-944b-7b4ac3d8364b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406769}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84951e54-c705-5b01-85eb-275b777e397c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161810" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+vw \\\\ {v}^{2} \\\\ w+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+vw,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2},\\,z(u,v,w)=w+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & w & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(2v-0) \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2v & =0 \\\\ v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=0$ defines the plane $v=0,$ i.e., $uw$-plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e37282e-8869-5ad2-9f01-e062a7d21846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v=2{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424153} {:answer/id #uuid "4a31e95b-7336-5699-a485-0b22a7563452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424154} {:answer/id #uuid "56201c08-96c0-58d8-9ed7-c9a2a6ecea11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424156} {:answer/id #uuid "7447254c-742a-5c7f-adb1-193a3168832a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424159} {:answer/id #uuid "7473d403-04c8-5521-92cd-20ba6d10f904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a31e95b-7336-5699-a485-0b22a7563452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424154} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a37030-5cd9-5e43-a291-e19dfc94c67f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424176}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b50230b8-d517-560a-9ede-747bedde3c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161811" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u}+v \\\\ v+w \\\\ vw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u}+v,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(v-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{u}(v-w) & =0 \\\\ v-w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-w=0$ defines the plane $v=w.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129271 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128848 :question/id #uuid "66d757d2-f2ad-5f0d-b4f1-8d426f4c8351" :question/math-academy-id "e-11579" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the Cartesian $uvw$-space onto the Cartesian $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx = 2u-v,\\qquad\ny = u+3v+w,\\qquad\nz = u+2v-w.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](images/6e/6e94e780387ccbba3d8b6a6aaf5f00fbf362cd8163837a8ea78ad31c18f3b1b3.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-3-2)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-10-2 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ec0914-5828-5c4a-81c9-ce52c1488ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323999} {:answer/id #uuid "350992e5-036c-535c-8a4b-6a5ab4c6fa16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324405} {:answer/id #uuid "81e74165-da06-5f93-ae92-aeef9db19857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325063} {:answer/id #uuid "a528987d-54c7-5615-ae6d-cbc6a9db0c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325376} {:answer/id #uuid "efd74025-7232-59ef-bfa0-45a2a9dfad28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a528987d-54c7-5615-ae6d-cbc6a9db0c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325376} :answer-field/id #uuid "60477555-dd44-54e5-9698-cd175b7154c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326385}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24830b7e-b0be-5ebb-9a57-2cd58c40cf8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39299" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +v\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=w.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $\\theta $ is a fixed constant." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c262fc-9bb7-50e9-9bd9-71220d222eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323988} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7284f1-bddd-57c4-bee7-4e81f853fddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2vw\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324235} {:answer/id #uuid "45d58fec-b6fd-54bf-819d-1320f8bbad5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324536} {:answer/id #uuid "5a92451e-4ced-5c86-9ad8-ed111fb7bb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4vw\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324719} {:answer/id #uuid "b55d6eaa-09cc-570c-89c0-c7bfca63934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b55d6eaa-09cc-570c-89c0-c7bfca63934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325531} :answer-field/id #uuid "785a8a4c-9863-5dc4-b03a-b4c3232d979e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326437}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "045d5f30-3ffc-5bb0-bb67-dd9e010fc71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39666" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4v \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw) \\\\ vw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=4v,\\,y(u,v,w)=3{u}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw),\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2w\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 6u & -2w\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) & -2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}6u & -2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ 0 & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4(6uv-0) \\\\  & =-24uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46d3639c-107c-558c-b1c4-6ddd7cb81df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{u}^{3}v-{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279222} {:answer/id #uuid "58a20343-ced5-57fc-b813-e98d7f250092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3{u}^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279406} {:answer/id #uuid "59f8ccb9-adf9-523b-b70b-b84e01b6cdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{3}-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279416} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0b239b-6293-5a6c-a399-4c20fbd9ba89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280232} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d8a949-98c9-53b2-a5c0-d91ecebaaf5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-u{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59f8ccb9-adf9-523b-b70b-b84e01b6cdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{3}-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279416} :answer-field/id #uuid "095a93fc-962d-5e7a-8222-89b127de9857" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba1e3545-8702-5a08-88b1-f7be09c82e4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161792" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u{v}^{2}+w,\\,y={u}^{3}+v,\\,z=uv.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 2uv & 1 \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a3a5e9-764a-5e8b-8b50-93add8b3fc05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279099} {:answer/id #uuid "723714f3-b594-566d-a56e-12b3c635b92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{w}(1+u)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279630} {:answer/id #uuid "790dd6b1-fb1b-55ce-8d23-99d906744d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-uw{e}^{u+w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279671} {:answer/id #uuid "b3690af9-7cf0-5336-b7d4-e7c30102fb21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}-3{e}^{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280174} {:answer/id #uuid "f77275e3-e3d3-5b1d-ad31-9113451a2bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3w{e}^{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "723714f3-b594-566d-a56e-12b3c635b92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{w}(1+u)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279630} :answer-field/id #uuid "d23defb7-252c-5ea7-b5af-33ad4ec74ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281204}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10460eec-f814-5d84-b88f-d89f8f558989" :question/math-academy-id "q-161793" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u{e}^{w} \\\\ w-u \\\\ w{e}^{u}-3v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u{e}^{w},\\,y(u,v,w)=w-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u}-3v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =u{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-3, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & 0 & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ w{e}^{u} & -3 & {e}^{u}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3({e}^{w}+u{e}^{w}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{w}(1+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07db5547-d099-5a39-ad82-abc875d51fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278662} {:answer/id #uuid "273f9725-21b0-51ad-aa3e-3681dbe69685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278942} {:answer/id #uuid "5643486d-15ec-545d-a303-f0e9cc3db1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279382} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7bc25d-f032-5290-9eff-ce5cbada276f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u-2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279426} {:answer/id #uuid "848acec7-1e41-5ca9-b95e-cefaaa4e11e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5643486d-15ec-545d-a303-f0e9cc3db1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279382} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea688683-0e2b-5e81-8438-0d34494b3330" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281250}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27f802ec-0d96-5c3f-9520-b80524648c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-161794" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ u+3w \\\\ -v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,z(u,v,w)=-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u-0 \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "555199b9-aced-5dd0-a7a4-1c3b529e0ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279370} {:answer/id #uuid "71d28527-0b0f-581a-b145-41b591816c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{uv}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279625} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf35eb4-7d06-504d-97d6-6d62c0d12660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279976} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2827ef-81bc-5319-a0af-3f83476042b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{w}-\\frac{1}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279995} {:answer/id #uuid "f447c433-d789-5722-828c-42983f30e64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f447c433-d789-5722-828c-42983f30e64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280731} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c51ca4-49aa-5089-b98e-4e0fed70679a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281007}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "590673d5-674e-5f87-8a3b-a26381077e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-161796" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w) \\\\ u+2w \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w),\\,y(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)=uv.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\frac{1}{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} & \\frac{1}{w} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}|+2(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(u-0)-2(1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{u}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b82058-2ed1-5ab3-8be0-f2ccd9e791be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2uv-2u{v}^{2}-vu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406759} {:answer/id #uuid "2b07851c-5666-5e08-93b4-ea52710578c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}-vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406761} {:answer/id #uuid "4e04d287-7eaa-501d-ba5e-706a25e1adcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406764} {:answer/id #uuid "78a6574d-a7a7-5dd3-9372-265b4fd8dfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2uv-2u{v}^{2}+vu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406765} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d7dba0-5d86-5a7b-97ed-55ed0cf1cb1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}+vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b07851c-5666-5e08-93b4-ea52710578c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}-vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406761} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7e9e1f-8201-5457-9ae8-866cb0a50e5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406770}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9eac1497-79c1-5ed3-be68-00cfa0edfef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-39710" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+vw,\\,y=2-uv,\\,z={v}^{2}-w.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & w & v \\\\ -v & -u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-u & 0 \\\\ 2v & -1\\end{aligned}|+(-v)(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}w & v \\\\ 2v & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(u-0)+v(-w-2{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}-vw.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c7d7c2-430d-5af8-9d05-fa5665e62862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424149} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2a26b3-3177-579e-877d-0fe20ffae536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424158} {:answer/id #uuid "9406cdae-72e2-5c66-8ad2-f1e16969ae1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424162} {:answer/id #uuid "af88273c-a488-5b6d-af48-680f8f2c6cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424163} {:answer/id #uuid "de9aadb1-15c7-5202-b107-ef067be5cce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c7d7c2-430d-5af8-9d05-fa5665e62862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424149} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d447ea2-8560-5077-b8b9-f3304d7a91d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424173}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03a9f960-3754-59ca-a7ce-a0ebf6a90fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-39294" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=v+w,\\,y=u+w,\\,z=u+v.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(0-1)+(1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146610e3-5c1c-5561-9ca3-9774666c6c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-a-b-c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424150} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fd2ac-fcea-50a9-92dd-45cccffc9e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-abc" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424155} {:answer/id #uuid "5938eea0-a7c5-5a7f-bf1f-a9fd0efaaf3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a+b+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424157} {:answer/id #uuid "7a38c64c-896b-5232-a5e4-465265d40434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "abc" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424161} {:answer/id #uuid "b5179334-ac94-5f42-8f5c-8c9ece45c7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a38c64c-896b-5232-a5e4-465265d40434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "abc" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424161} :answer-field/id #uuid "3708f8e2-7d98-51bd-8b21-20ee27c9855a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424174}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fc3f590-a25e-51e5-80e8-0d05b569e022" :question/math-academy-id "q-39664" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=au,\\,y=bv,\\,z=cw.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $a$, $b$ and $c$ are fixed constants." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =a & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =b & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =a(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}b & 0 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =a(bc-0) \\\\  & =abc.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128853 :question/id #uuid "e46cfe46-965d-5c28-90ed-060ec39751a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12376" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/14/14df7999d11afbe9002e05521d71f5008f2b49250238a714fada7dcc4aeb86d2.svg) express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n![](images/00/009865419103717553af1a73b3de0ac958a76eb2fdc9c95467236581d3ad0e11.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n![](images/82/829d32a5563a183ecc1f646b20fba584838dffb9ce67dcc0e014d2a90bcb7add.svg)\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region ![](images/f7/f7c8d853846850d1bf4afd67f7544f3f3c3756eb0de33ee5c27985e83449997d.svg)\n\n![](images/e4/e447e688e0f3e2c0411129e8d1baf4a900008173443f5392d98af52a27eb285b.png)\n\nNotice that the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) represents the disk of radius ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) centered at ![](images/37/37cb790b6a640626cb093bf7ee969ddc9aafd1eb91c86d96a5339e6df2555fca.svg) \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n![](images/df/dfe1b368d21a4745d71b5fe084246ce0a16cb6a85ad983598442d0dbde0ed591.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ![](images/ae/ae3d9424cc536ab651dc8732546bd8bfea96901ce744793ec8797c02fc446ba4.svg) in polar coordinates:\n![](images/11/11d7065eda072cefb2c9ab63e679d3a0e7103a146a37b21440f0ff2e4f393369.svg) \n\nSince ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have ![](images/3f/3f7ce8399dec23cec965849d4623642dcaf947ca8500a837ce8ef771a42b7ee3.svg)\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) and ![](images/b9/b98533dee87a7bd8ed4d593a2aabd517621d6ab96e40a5b2ab4a8ac1923ddac5.svg) First of all, ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) can vary from ![](images/63/632079982c24b29d4de5e322230ed61f106b1715de40fdb8b6879e982a9f3955.svg) (at the origin) to ![](images/4b/4b56a88cc6e7ba7c13d83cf18ea0f759a70af4d07186f2be1c558e25589e1c7c.svg) (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/52/522df0e95e36cd67f34ffcc0ec4757c35749eab5d5137215c456c8b7e90994ae.png)\n\nSecondly, ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) can vary between ![](images/64/64fd2741e70e8f1d2a64c58d05dc7124c6e6e94a5d7dea30124e4200e8c61e75.svg) (the negative ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis) and ![](images/b7/b76ea8adee01315895dd8c61949a5c8f28235929b53bbff59c58856e04ebd63f.svg) (the positive ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis):\n\n![](images/6a/6ad24b638877941296df25869f8b0ee6f9dc1e73a779a8a0da01de35d9f74f42.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates ![](images/49/4974a18e033f45691b0d3cab8bd7a16744004e4303f44184bb7045a7c64e6f8c.svg) the region can be expressed as \n![](images/9c/9cab9c4a9d446ee9fd9794a9455d46b63df1b53ce505236d7b91816cc6496890.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n![](images/5d/5d809200294cb2ddc88d35101eb6fc449902fce4ebb0b0fd9c883a32ee5c3b0c.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1091c5-d81a-565f-a281-b2fb0d00c973" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b681cf26-ad41-5653-bce9-580974381de0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6\\cos θ}r^{2}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126579} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4598f3-014d-5222-9a41-c4efd38e3ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126580} {:answer/id #uuid "e88b4c4a-b486-568a-b20f-a07fa3b118c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126581} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6bf261-6b14-56f1-8c08-c0943ec14aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126582} {:answer/id #uuid "3f871b80-da7e-53c9-96ef-432946627099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6\\cos θ}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126583}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2356402a-db2e-5c19-a235-969c763d3490" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126578}] :question/id #uuid "46644fa9-6cbb-532b-913d-789dd58285a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161068" :question/problem "If $D = {(x, y): (x - 3)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 9}$, then $∬_(D)(x^{2} - y^{2})dA =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88c5f797-a9d1-57fa-9b5c-199c1707c699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127250} {:answer/id #uuid "a5444d7b-d73e-51e7-acb5-9a93ee60e11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2\\cos θ}r^{2}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127251} {:answer/id #uuid "bae770e3-6a1c-54a7-b9f0-d75d08314408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}r^{2}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127252} {:answer/id #uuid "65798862-d116-58c2-b3af-89c7218b8ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}rdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127253} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb4e9b6-29bc-533d-854e-c1558288c702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\cos θ\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127254}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a553d3-061a-5f0f-8a06-f47ae65fdaea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127249}] :question/id #uuid "e8b65659-f286-503b-84ce-d0959f9a4ff0" :question/math-academy-id "q-38077" :question/problem "If $D = {(x, y): (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1, y \\ge 0}$, then $∬_(D)x\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03227120-1691-54be-9581-ee3778fd0f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275506} {:answer/id #uuid "429cfe66-9dc1-57e0-aefc-d63e0ab79341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276063} {:answer/id #uuid "57adbd5c-f9ae-58db-9b1f-56917ec83b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276261} {:answer/id #uuid "a1990493-12c6-5eb8-8f46-77be04071c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276933} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b0b3be-8afe-561a-847e-c27f91b19430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1990493-12c6-5eb8-8f46-77be04071c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276933} :answer-field/id #uuid "93143ee8-8f7b-58c3-a62c-f096bb59115c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277985}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "680e85ad-70a0-5f2a-915e-2d6c3749cdbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161065" :question/problem "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1},$ then ${∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/f0/f0417011f46d3860b7de86fe652a7d80dd9787cb05c2057601b41c17e0c2b423.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/3f/3f4bb1428a6cc9b7e191e1904636d654145d9a1f2fb1dd40843d8cd878194772.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/d6/d6f4e054767650cd2a3d348aee8210af421e2df21792d79f42c5a58b5f4e82e6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3a50e4-5411-52b3-a962-4147981c80b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276132} {:answer/id #uuid "971cd55b-3477-5ef3-9184-3dd244f49480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276833} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a719b4-a204-5304-94dc-010ad58340ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276970} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf4833c-b73d-57fe-b477-fadb221b09e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277159} {:answer/id #uuid "cb63beea-6aa8-51c4-97e1-f8b665be72c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5a719b4-a204-5304-94dc-010ad58340ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276970} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3661af2-fdbf-5479-8627-1d09d5073cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6beb18bc-dc57-5d1c-8540-705d9f63089f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161067" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/86036fd86096355b969da695f812c9bf835880edd68f7991183b70a6234e3eb4.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/9e/9ef22dcc15175664331d989bd50d9a8d60a354a0780dbaf9465caca04f28c2d9.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/f7/f724daf8321195815c9e413aab2106c611bc9d2e9653939266fc828aebeca68c.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b90531f-8c48-528f-be8c-e6dbd51abb0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275998} {:answer/id #uuid "52d5e922-bccb-5fce-b300-691e090faea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276212} {:answer/id #uuid "7df546c6-3376-55f8-94c7-e5be74bd7136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276624} {:answer/id #uuid "acefb97e-6196-52ba-a16d-9390374236cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277032} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3e9490-2677-5e30-aba6-03b5cf96c58e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b90531f-8c48-528f-be8c-e6dbd51abb0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275998} :answer-field/id #uuid "5007391b-6a4b-5cf3-b468-d5880451e5ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4206eea6-b522-5853-935f-b0d344e48a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161070" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,\\,y\\le 0\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d8/d882bf70ecb7b94eee0eeeffbf63874ca8607268167f96278deb4678ec3b827a.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/60/605d9ce1c9030d1ac83c0a320bd648b42893f222279dd2d56b111acd9957c202.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/f3/f37e34042338af2b1ba5f0c214ebf046eeba068b0b3238b662910a868c069ee4.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0697c65a-b335-5808-858f-4aefa5836f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348505} {:answer/id #uuid "22696687-a1a0-5b4e-9fc2-4b6660080ccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348743} {:answer/id #uuid "48190f6b-8b75-5fe3-9e3c-8380e3b6d891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349098} {:answer/id #uuid "94569d81-6196-574d-b5dc-b8a14b560fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349772} {:answer/id #uuid "f0453531-dd2e-538b-9315-fe63fc1a8163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48190f6b-8b75-5fe3-9e3c-8380e3b6d891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349098} :answer-field/id #uuid "114ca2dd-599d-5dcd-8f3a-3c67d4dd384e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "776c75d9-2096-5d91-bc10-713520516ef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-83502" :question/problem "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,x\\ge 0},$ then ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/96/968419bad65410864c75e8abe766d21ac4d4acd835c73ea84118cf21ac066f79.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/2f/2fe33fb6e1be8b6107cb667e9453613af906bd4da7d67f9d9323d49cce5ae06c.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/e2/e2b5e7b4433cbfb50ac7a9f991e4e4bc0cf8deecfefeeef0a4c9606ab6183cce.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c77ea9bc-2c0d-561d-a364-eb7c29c4f841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394506} {:answer/id #uuid "ddadced8-2143-5670-94ef-c5d98f93192e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394507} {:answer/id #uuid "8dff8e1c-ebb3-52f2-8a15-a57dc0d7bbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394508} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5f2fd4-bdfe-5a77-bfc6-f8822f475f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394509} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9e64dc-cb50-57f4-a11f-0e91717eba0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e9e64dc-cb50-57f4-a11f-0e91717eba0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394510} :answer-field/id #uuid "70a6a823-aaff-53a1-8971-3dd47ec005cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394505}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f578d29d-0823-5f21-864f-baa92f133476" :question/math-academy-id "q-83501" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\le 0\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/61/617a69d44661d03e1c170b0c397d11f6437a7222c286df5023154371ec90ab4d.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/2d/2d50f2375c071d8bfb4c5c58a836bef76a70557c2593d4df9acf48f9f0e7527b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/98/98e9c8442c17cbc77083c5dc55a9d74324befa05e3cd47e1c65c38cd510c08c6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8daaa863-4ed5-528c-8dbf-f411602a18b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394547} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff14fdd-eefc-5322-8abf-2722d438a026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394548} {:answer/id #uuid "b2216cb8-dda6-543b-bea0-78a155f39c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394549} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9fdf78-2c9f-596d-b102-140d5e888ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394550} {:answer/id #uuid "49a2ca2d-1ffc-59a5-8706-fbb1eada3fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8daaa863-4ed5-528c-8dbf-f411602a18b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394547} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a886ac-bf1c-524b-87ca-5608d2086121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394546}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b9a1961-cfec-5ea0-aa17-358f76216410" :question/math-academy-id "q-83955" :question/problem "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,x\\le 0},$ then ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/32/32c4bf490df51121ddf1575df8211033185d392fd5b08519971723b820da191f.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/b1/b1e2eac0041d0b0cb802561b3fb83fd8fab1b940cb6c42f698879837c06cbff0.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/19/193601785ff94605d12b91b7cab1e7390af532ba1912530bd8d3f492324e2788.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044b6779-39b2-56a9-9df0-496dff81f1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394554} {:answer/id #uuid "82c50572-40d6-5a44-ab8f-fb0fe55d5d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394555} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c95244-f85c-5475-84d2-8042553696dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394556} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d768e9-aaaa-5ff9-92ba-2ecd8298c7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394557} {:answer/id #uuid "af086492-0d1d-5948-872e-742615c22b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3c95244-f85c-5475-84d2-8042553696dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394556} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a0f488-8a01-545c-97d1-1d9ad1edfb32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394553}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1de76f2-2bbf-5f6f-8001-65ebe80eec1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83956" :question/problem "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\le 0},$ then ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/bf/bf84eed655256e63439ca907610c4b0b022c6c480d5ab0b6c12d6826af9f0907.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/6d/6de2b6e2b9925577f8cf0961f8466707f770ddca175a04dac9b592542aac16e8.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/4a/4a56d4023b03b40b4c1be5f22809963d6c745fd90ed3d860cf69d555b3e869ae.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93b12cef-0de3-5eae-b613-57a5a24bfe9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436968} {:answer/id #uuid "98177725-7d29-51e2-8e0c-74639d3d9e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436969} {:answer/id #uuid "9d796d42-9e19-53b0-bd6f-f39374f45098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436970} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e3648e-b68d-5ef6-9e9c-21d21e9cf0e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436971} {:answer/id #uuid "d9abcc56-cbcc-55a7-98d1-f48f8a4fdcb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d796d42-9e19-53b0-bd6f-f39374f45098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436970} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e485133-85f6-59c3-b320-b44902b78672" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436973}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f9560d7-409d-5ebf-b57a-a19bcb1f8e6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161064" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 9\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/f0/f0a5627965f71f6eff869a45b1b575b892a67ebba9b1ec969f5dc38ba84cb250.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $3$ centered at $(0,-3).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/75/7515fffda1580baa332df454e7fe53a60f70944215f13d83e0e2269a0101dba6.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =2\\pi $ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Not Centered at the Origin"}]}
